# Decision-log writer

[Script](../scripts/log.sh). Append one sanitized row to the decision trail
defined by [Show me your work](../SKILL.md). Requires Bash and standard Unix
utilities. The caller's assigned path and write/commit limits still apply.

## Run

Resolve the script from the installed show-me-your-work directory. Keep the
repository working directory and quote each argument as data.

```bash
"{% if harness == "claude" %}{{ skill_root }}{% else %}<show-me-your-work skill directory>{% endif %}/scripts/log.sh" <logfile> <phase> <decision> <why> <evidence> <result>
```

Exactly six arguments are required. The skill owns the meaning of each field,
when to log, run boundaries and evidence rules. The
[header template](decision-log-template.tsv) shows the TSV column order.

The script stamps `ts` in UTC, creates a missing parent directory and appends
the header when the file is absent or empty. It replaces tabs, newlines and
carriage returns inside cells with spaces, then prefixes cells starting with
`=`, `+`, `-` or `@` with a single quote to prevent spreadsheet formulas.
It appends one row; it does not edit existing rows or review their truth.

## Result and failures

A successful append exits 0 without a success payload. Wrong argument count
prints usage to stderr and exits 1. Filesystem/utility failures remain nonzero
errors. The script has no `--help` flag or automatic retry.

After an interrupted or failed write, inspect the log before appending again.
There is no lock or deduplication: repeating a command appends another row.
The caller owns concurrent writers. Do not use the script to promote scratch
evidence into product files or commit it without covering authority.

A manual `printf` append remains possible under the skill's existing workflow,
but it must preserve the same column, escaping and formula-safety rules.
Execution uses the client's ordinary permission prompt; no new script
permission is granted by this reference.
